WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.133
-Kelly...
-Hi. Hi.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:04.567
-Great to see you.
Always fun to see you.

00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:06.801
Have we danced together before?
I feel like we have.

00:00:06.801 --> 00:00:07.934
-We've danced together
at parties.

00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:10.133
-Right? At parties. Yeah.
-Yeah. Absolutely.

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.367
-You're a good dancer.
-You're a great dancer.

00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:14.968
-Really?
-Yes, I think you know you are.

00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:16.000
-I'm not, though.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.901
-No, you're like
a quadruple threat.

00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:19.167
-No, I just got exposed,

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:21.167
because I was at a concert
and I was dad-dancing.

00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:23.501
-Yeah, but dad dancing is
still cool dancing

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:26.300
because it's dancing that
led to babies.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.234
-Okay, good. I'll take that.
Thank you.

00:00:28.234 --> 00:00:30.567
-I mean, you know,
as long as you know how to

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:32.267
dance one way,
that's all that really matters.

00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:34.300
-That's what I'm talking about.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.300
Congrats.
"Live Wire" is out today!

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:37.467
-Thank you.
-It's out.

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:40.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:42.801
Congratulations. I love --
-Thank you.

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:45.133
-I want to get into all of it,
because I love the cover --

00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:47.400
-Oh, okay.
Can we talk about the cover?

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.167
-Yes, please.
-Okay, so, I picked this photo.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.000
And this is my demented brain.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.467
But tell me this is not
the genius marketing strategy.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:58.267
I thought people might think
this is Sarah Jessica Parker

00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:01.033
and maybe think it's
a book about "Sex and the City"

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:03.167
and accidentally buy it.

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:04.567
-You're so funny.

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:05.801
-Tell me that's not
a great strategy.

00:01:05.801 --> 00:01:08.000
-No, that's not --
You did not think like that.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.100
-I did think like that.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.467
-But then why would you put --

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:11.734
Why didn't you just put
Sarah Jessica -- You know her.

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:13.100
-Because people are going to
think, "Oh, my God.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.434
That's so amazing.

00:01:14.434 --> 00:01:18.767
Kelly Ripa wrote a book about
Sarah Jessica Parker.

00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:21.701
-I love the colors.
I love the -- It pops.

00:01:21.701 --> 00:01:23.434
I mean, when you hold it when
you're on the subway,

00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:24.734
you're like, "What's up?"

00:01:24.734 --> 00:01:26.033
They're like, "He gets it."

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:27.834
-Carry it on the subway?
-"He gets it."

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:30.634
Yeah. You know what I'm saying?
-Yes.

00:01:30.634 --> 00:01:34.334
And I like that that's
how you walk on the subway.

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:36.534
-Yeah, I don't even stand in
the -- I walk in between cars

00:01:36.534 --> 00:01:37.901
and just got to
get to my destination.

00:01:37.901 --> 00:01:40.267
-And you just have to say,
"I love 'Sex and the City.'"

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.400
-They're like, "Oh, my God."

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.234
I love the honesty of it.

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:46.067
I think the humor is great.
-Thanks.

00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:49.100
-I love also
"Long-Winded Short Stories,"

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.167
so very self-deprecating,
as well.

00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:51.634
-Well, you know me.

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:56.434
So, believe it or not, they cut
200 pages out of this book.

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:58.968
I'm not kidding.
You're welcome, America.

00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:00.400
-No, I believe that.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.567
Everything here is all --
This is what you want.

00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:04.067
This is all the juicy stories.

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:05.701
-It was a lot harder
than I thought.

00:02:05.701 --> 00:02:08.000
I thought that because
I read books

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.801
that I could somehow write them.

00:02:09.801 --> 00:02:13.701
And spoiler alert --
they're real, real different.

00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:16.100
-Yeah. It's very hard to
write a book.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.267
-It was really hard.

00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:19.534
And, you know,
there were times I realized

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:23.801
I'm such a poor typist that
I wound up writing it freehand

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:26.100
half the time
and then transposing it.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.400
It was so -- It was difficult,
and I had to really --

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.868
-But they asked you --
Your publisher said,

00:02:32.868 --> 00:02:34.033
"Hey, do you want
a ghostwriter,

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:35.167
someone that can
write this for you?"

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:36.801
And you said, "No,
if I'm doing this --"

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:40.033
-I've got to do it myself.
-Yeah. Good for you.

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:42.334
-Good for me, but also dumb me,

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:45.167
because I Googled
a thousand times how to use

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.167
a semicolon, how to use a colon.

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:49.067
I still don't know.

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:51.067
-Yeah, I don't know
about that either.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:52.734
There would be dashes
in all my books.

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:53.968
-I graduated high school.

00:02:53.968 --> 00:02:55.501
I should know this [bleep]

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:58.300
Sorry.
-Oh, my God.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.968
-I should know.
-You should know.

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:03.834
-Texting my kids
at 2:00 a.m. like,

00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:05.334
"Yo, know how to
use a semicolon?"

00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:10.267
-Yeah. There's so many
funny, great stories here,

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:12.634
and a couple have
already made headlines.

00:03:12.634 --> 00:03:15.033
One that we could say.

00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:17.334
There was one about
you and your husband...

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:18.400
uh...
[ Clears throat ]

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.734
-Yeah.
-...yeah, having --

00:03:19.734 --> 00:03:21.334
-Yeah, intercourse.
-Thank you.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.000
-Yeah. Sorry.
-Don't make me say it.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.667
-Of the sexual kind.
-Of the sexual kind, yes.

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:29.701
-It's actually a very --
It sounds so crass

00:03:29.701 --> 00:03:33.167
and it sounds so gross
and salacious.

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:35.968
And it is all of those things.

00:03:35.968 --> 00:03:37.400
-Oh, thank you.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.801
-But at the heart of it, it's
about -- We had just had a baby.

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:45.834
And I think that a lot of women
that have just had babies

00:03:45.834 --> 00:03:49.667
are never quite sure
what goes where ever again

00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:54.033
or how it's going to work
or will it ever feel normal?

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:58.400
And I was one of those women,
and unfortunately

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.801
for my poor husband,
Mark Consuelos,

00:04:01.801 --> 00:04:04.300
who is a great hero
in this story --

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.033
-I love Mark. He is -- Yes.
-He really is a hero.

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:08.734
No wonder he's been
promoting this book so much.

00:04:08.734 --> 00:04:09.934
He's the hero of the story.

00:04:09.934 --> 00:04:13.467
-He comes off fantastically cool
in every story.

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:15.434
He comes off like, "Wow!"

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:18.234
-You know, I had --
Sorry, everyone,

00:04:18.234 --> 00:04:20.267
if you're having
your dessert right now.

00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:23.234
But he -- I had an ovarian cyst.

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:25.467
I didn't know
I had an ovarian cyst.

00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:32.534
And during the "love-making,"
I jumped up, fell,

00:04:32.534 --> 00:04:33.767
and hit my head.

00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:35.934
And he did not know what to do.

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:37.367
He panicked.

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.100
-So everyone -- The story --
I just want everyone to know,

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.801
the headline is, he was so
good in bed that you passed out.

00:04:42.801 --> 00:04:44.334
-Right. The headline that has

00:04:44.334 --> 00:04:47.300
gotten picked up everywhere
is that my husband

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.367
is so, so -- I mean, who knows?

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:53.000
-Yeah.
-He's so --

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.133
[ Funk music playing ]
Yes, thank you.

00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:56.267
-Yeah.
-Yes. Cue the porno music.

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:57.934
-That you just passed out.

00:04:57.934 --> 00:04:59.367
and had to be
rushed to the hospital.

00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:04.901
-Right. But the story is that I
passed out, I hit my head.

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:09.200
He calls 911 and then
dresses me incredibly badly.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.734
-You have to tell this,

00:05:10.734 --> 00:05:13.100
because this is the part of
the story that made me laugh.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.267
What were you wearing?

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:19.634
-He snakes me into
a Capezio ballet leotard.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:22.534
-He didn't have
sweatpants around or anything?

00:05:22.534 --> 00:05:23.801
-Yes, I did.

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:26.467
I had a whole closet full of
sweatpants and T-shirts.

00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:27.934
-Alright, so,
you were wearing a leotard.

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:30.400
-Snakes me into
a ballet leotard.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:35.000
Then he puts on
his side-snap soccer pants

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.167
in case we want to pick up
a game somewhere.

00:05:37.167 --> 00:05:38.801
-He's got to rip them right off.

00:05:38.801 --> 00:05:42.033
-And then the
pièce de résistance

00:05:42.033 --> 00:05:47.801
are the patent-leather red
Manolo Blahnik platform heels.

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.801
-You're being put on
the back of an ambulance...

00:05:49.801 --> 00:05:52.167
-I'm being put in
the back of an ambulance --

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:53.834
-...wearing heels
and soccer pants.

00:05:53.834 --> 00:05:55.968
-Wearing heels,
and they're like --

00:05:55.968 --> 00:06:02.267
you know, the paramedics are
like, "A prostitute has died.

00:06:02.267 --> 00:06:04.033
We don't know..."
-Oh, my God.

00:06:04.033 --> 00:06:07.334
That was one of
your titles of the book.

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:08.367
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:09.667
-So, yeah.

00:06:09.667 --> 00:06:12.767
So, if you like stories about
sex and romance...

00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:14.100
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.367
No, it's also stories
about family,

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:19.501
but also your career,
getting started,

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:21.267
I mean, and how hard it is.

00:06:21.267 --> 00:06:24.601
-It's hard, yeah.
It was a labor of love.

00:06:24.601 --> 00:06:26.067
I keep saying
it's like giving birth,

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:28.067
but nobody offers you
any painkillers

00:06:28.067 --> 00:06:29.701
at any time during the process.

00:06:29.701 --> 00:06:31.501
-Of writing a book.
-Of writing a book.

00:06:31.501 --> 00:06:34.968
And, you know,
you were remarking about

00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:38.033
the final chapter in the book,
and I want you to know that

00:06:38.033 --> 00:06:40.968
you're going to blink --
And people told us this.

00:06:40.968 --> 00:06:42.567
You're going to blink,
and your children

00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:44.734
are going to be grown
and out of the nest,

00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:46.968
and you don't know
where the time went.

00:06:46.968 --> 00:06:48.767
And that's really what
the story is.

00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:50.467
It's about endurance,

00:06:50.467 --> 00:06:54.133
endurance as a couple,
endurance as a work person,

00:06:54.133 --> 00:06:55.701
endurance in your careers,

00:06:55.701 --> 00:06:58.767
endurance in
just raising your family.

00:06:58.767 --> 00:07:00.968
It's a story of endurance.

00:07:00.968 --> 00:07:02.801
-Yeah, and keep going.

00:07:02.801 --> 00:07:05.100
-And make sure
you pick out clothes

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:08.200
just in case you pass out
while having sex,

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.667
in case of an emergency.

00:07:09.667 --> 00:07:12.033
-I do love that --
When I pick up a book,

00:07:12.033 --> 00:07:14.300
I sometimes like to go to
the last line,

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:15.567
which I probably shouldn't do.

00:07:15.567 --> 00:07:17.934
-No, I do that all the time.
-But it's beautiful.

00:07:17.934 --> 00:07:19.400
It's a good last line.
Can I say it or --

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:20.734
-Yeah, of course. Say it.

00:07:20.734 --> 00:07:23.901
-It's Mark saying to you,
"This is just the beginning."

00:07:23.901 --> 00:07:28.567
-Yeah. I told you,
he's the hero of this story.

00:07:28.567 --> 00:07:29.834
-It's like,
"Look at what we made.

00:07:29.834 --> 00:07:32.601
Look at our life.
Our kids are now in school.

00:07:32.601 --> 00:07:34.400
They're happy. They're healthy.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:35.968
And let's just enjoy everything.

00:07:35.968 --> 00:07:38.534
-They're pissed that Kid Cudi is
here and they are not here.

00:07:38.534 --> 00:07:41.534
-Yeah, they're a little upset.

00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:44.634
But one thing I learned
from you from this book

00:07:44.634 --> 00:07:46.601
is that we have
something in common.

00:07:46.601 --> 00:07:49.234
On our birthdays, we both have
Carvel ice-cream cakes.

00:07:49.234 --> 00:07:50.467
-We sure do.

00:07:50.467 --> 00:07:52.501
-And I figured, since it's kind
of your birthday,

00:07:52.501 --> 00:07:55.534
it's publishing day,
your book is now out,

00:07:55.534 --> 00:07:59.968
your book's birthday,
I got a Carvel Fudgie the Whale.

00:07:59.968 --> 00:08:01.400
-Thank you. My favorite.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:05.100
-I'm going to light it,
and I want you to make a wish.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.400
There we go.
I'm going to light it,

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.400
and then make a wish,
and it's going to come true.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:09.734
Don't tell me what it is.
-Okay.

00:08:09.734 --> 00:08:11.300
-Yep.
[ Drumroll ]

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.133
Oh, my gosh. I love it
right there. Here you go.

00:08:13.133 --> 00:08:14.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:14.901 --> 00:08:16.734
-Are we doing it?
-Kelly Ripa right here.

00:08:16.734 --> 00:08:18.634
Congrats on the book.
-Thanks, baby.

00:08:18.634 --> 00:08:20.734
-I feel like you deserve it.

00:08:20.734 --> 00:08:24.234
When we come back,
we'll talk about

00:08:24.234 --> 00:08:26.667
"Live Wire:
Long-Winded Short Stories."

00:08:26.667 --> 00:08:28.133
We're gonna play a fun game.
Come on back.

